dispatch: eager launches of an opted-in thread publish their fetch-queue entries together

Each eager program launch wrote its prefetch-queue entry to the prefetcher's L1 with a strict-order MMIO
write, a stall of about 11 us on the host. A thread that calls tt_metal_fetch_queue_batching(n) (extern "C")
now holds up to n entries of its one-shot launches on a one-device mesh and writes them in one block when the
batch is full, after 50 us, before any other fetch-queue write (all devices), before a fetch-queue wait or
wrap, at a trace capture, in every socket poll and wait, in ~SystemMemoryManager, and when
tt_metal_flush_fetch_queues() is called. Threads that do not opt in, multi-device meshes, trace stalls and
the cached ring behave as before. A thread that opts in must call tt_metal_flush_fetch_queues() before it
blocks outside the command queue.

